  "question": "**Case Study 5: Robotics – Joint Angle Limits for Safety**<br /><br />A humanoid robot has an elbow joint that can flex such that the joint angle $\\alpha$ (in radians) is given by $\\alpha = \\cos^{-1}\\left(1-\\frac{x^2}{2L^2}\\right)$ where $x$ is the separation between the arm ends and $L$ is arm segment length.<br /><br />(a) For $x=L$, calculate $\\alpha$.<br /><br />(b) For $x=\\sqrt2L$, calculate $\\alpha$.<br /><br />(c) Find $x$ when $\\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "(a) $\\alpha=\\cos^{-1}(1-1/2)=\\cos^{-1}(1/2)=\\pi/3$.<br /><br />(b) $\\alpha=\\cos^{-1}(1-1)=\\cos^{-1}(0)=\\pi/2$.<br /><br />(c) If $\\alpha=\\pi/3$, then $1-\\frac{x^2}{2L^2}=1/2$.<br /><br /> Hence $x^2=L^2$ so $x=L$.",
  "explanation_image": [],
